 {"index":13,"url":"https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/activation-model-scanner","title":"Google AMS: Activation-based Model Scanner (repo README, primary source for sigma bands)",
  "summary":"Primary AMS source re-fetched 2026-09-19 per plan Phase 4.3. Sigma bands, thresholds, concept definitions, method (optimal layer 40-80% depth, difference-of-means direction, pooled-sigma separation), Tier-1/Tier-2 scans, limitations. NOTE: README says thresholds calibrated on 15 models across 4 architectures (dep summary said 14 configurations; README is primary).",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"| Instruction-tuned (Llama, Gemma, Qwen) | 4.7-8.4 | PASS | Abliterated models | 3.3 | WARNING | \"Uncensored\" fine-tunes | 1.1-1.3 | CRITICAL | Base model (no safety training) | 0.7 | CRITICAL","locator":"README.md, 'Core Insight' table (sigma bands: 4.7-8.4 / 3.3 / 1.1-1.3 / 0.7)"},
   {"quote":"PASS (>3.5), WARNING (2.0-3.5), CRITICAL (<2.0)","locator":"README.md, 'Two-Tier Scanning' Tier 1 thresholds"},
   {"quote":"Thresholds were calibrated on 15 models across 4 architectures","locator":"README.md, 'Technical Details > Threshold Selection'"},
   {"quote":"Extract hidden states at the optimal layer (typically 40-80% depth). Compute direction vector: v = mean(h_positive) - mean(h_negative). Measure class separation: separation = (mu+ - mu-) / sigma_pooled","locator":"README.md, 'Technical Details > Activation Extraction'"},
   {"quote":"Scan times of 10-40 seconds quoted in the paper are based on NVIDIA A100/L4 hardware.","locator":"README.md, 'Platform Requirements'"},
   {"quote":"Tier 2: Identity Verification (Baseline required) ... Compares direction vector orientation (cosine similarity >0.7) ... Catches subtle modifications, abliteration, and weight substitution","locator":"README.md, 'Two-Tier Scanning' Tier 2"},
   {"quote":"Base models: Pre-trained models without instruction tuning naturally have weaker safety directions. AMS may flag these as unsafe even though they were never intended to have safety training. ... False negatives: Sophisticated attacks that preserve safety directions while introducing vulnerabilities may not be detected.","locator":"README.md, 'Limitations'"}]},
 {"index":14,"url":"https://research.google/pubs/ams-detecting-unsafe-and-tampered-language-models-via-activation-analysis/","title":"AMS paper abstract: Detecting Safety Training Modification in Language Models via Activation Analysis",
  "summary":"AMS technical report abstract: four-class taxonomy of safety-training modifications, sigma values per class, executed structural-vs-behavioral correlations, and the class-4 failure mode. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"We further measure behavioral compliance on 20 stratified JailbreakBench prompts per model and find that sigma on the harmful-content concept predicts compliance with Pearson r=-0.546 (p=0.043); the rank-order Spearman correlation is weaker (rho=-0.423, p=0.13). The structural signal predicts behavior directionally but with meaningful noise.","locator":"research.google/pubs/... abstract, para 2"},
   {"quote":"Mechanistic analysis identifies a four-class taxonomy of safety-training modifications distinguished by activation-space signature: 1) training removal collapses cluster separation (e.g., base models, Dolphin variants: 0.5-1.4sigma); 2) weight-orthogonalization-style abliteration both collapses separation and rotates the refusal direction (Llama-3.1-abliterated: sigma=3.33, direction cos sim 0.30); 3) rotation-without-collapse abliteration preserves cluster separation while rotating the refusal direction (Gemma-2-9b-abliterated: sigma=4.54, direction cos sim 0.84); and 4) behavioral fine-tuning that preserves both magnitude and direction (DarkIdol-1.2-Uncensored: sigma=5.45, direction preserved, 97% behavioral compliance).","locator":"research.google/pubs/... abstract, para 3"},
   {"quote":"and 4) Class is undetectable by activation-only probing and represents a documented failure mode of the approach. We discuss threshold calibration, limitations of single-run measurement, and the open problem of detecting behavioral-only safety modifications.","locator":"research.google/pubs/... abstract, para 3"},
   {"quote":"confidence intervals on sigma point estimates have median width 3.4sigma and a substantial fraction of cells cross the PASS threshold under resampling","locator":"research.google/pubs/... abstract, para 2"}]},
 {"index":15,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25750","title":"SafeVec / RAS: white-box refusal-alignment scoring (arXiv:2606.25750)",
  "summary":"SafeVec extracts layer-wise refusal directions from a reference aligned model, selects stable layer windows, scores any target model with RAS (Refusal Alignment Score, calibrated 0-100); separates aligned vs uncensored/abliterated across Llama/Gemma/Qwen and tracks output-level ASR. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"SafeVec first extracts layer-wise refusal directions from a safety-aligned reference model, then selects stable layer windows where safe and unsafe behaviors are separable, and finally scores a target model by measuring whether its hidden states align with these refusal directions under unsafe and jailbreak prompts.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"Across Llama, Gemma, and Qwen model families, RAS separates aligned models from uncensored and abliterated variants, tracks output-level attack success rate, and is substantially faster than judge-based evaluation.","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
 {"index":16,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.01854","title":"Has This Checkpoint Been Abliterated? A Two-Signal Audit and Its Failure Map (arXiv:2607.01854)",
  "summary":"Checkpoint-level 2-class audit: reference-anchored activation refusal-gap plus weight-recovery energy; z-sum AUROC 0.95 over 273 checkpoints (57 abliterations vs 37 benign edits); leave-one-family-out balanced accuracy 0.89; failure map (spoofed reference; white-box owner). Fetched 2026-09-19 (v2 dated 18 Aug 2026).",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"On a 273-checkpoint registry spanning Qwen, DeepSeek-distilled Qwen, Llama, and Gemma, their z-sum separates 57 public abliterations from 37 benign fine-tunes, merges, and instruction-tunes at AUROC 0.95, significantly above either signal alone (0.84, 0.90), and a Youden-calibrated threshold transfers to held-out families at balanced accuracy 0.89 (FPR 0.11), missing only 4 of 57.","locator":"arXiv abstract, v2"},
   {"quote":"The weight-recovery energy is E1 = 1/|W| sum_{m in W} sigma1^2(DeltaWm) / sum_i sigma_i^2(DeltaWm) in [0, 1], DeltaWm = Wm_b - Wm_c","locator":"PDF Section 3 (grep extract)"},
   {"quote":"so we z-standardize each on a reference population and sum: s(Mc) = z(-rho) + z(E1). The score is threshold-free; the standardization is transductive","locator":"PDF Section 3 (grep extract)"},
   {"quote":"a spoofed reference evades both axes with no training (DeltaW=0, rho=1 by construction), and a white-box owner trains a checkpoint past the threshold while it stays guard-unsafe and coherent","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
 {"index":17,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09674","title":"The Hidden Dimensions of LLM Alignment: A Multi-Dimensional Analysis of Orthogonal Safety Directions (Pan et al. 2025)",
  "summary":"Safety-aligned behavior jointly controlled by multi-dimensional directions on Llama-3-8B: a dominant refusal direction plus multiple smaller orthogonal directions (hypothetical narrative, role-playing); trigger-token mitigation. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":["Wenbo Pan","Zhichao Liu","Qiguang Chen","Xiangyang Zhou","Haining Yu","Xiaohua Jia"],"year":2025,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"we discover that safety-aligned behavior is jointly controlled by multi-dimensional directions. Namely, we study the vector space of representation shifts during safety fine-tuning on Llama 3 8B for refusing jailbreaks. By studying orthogonal directions in the space, we first find that a dominant direction governs the model's refusal behavior, while multiple smaller directions represent distinct and interpretable features like hypothetical narrative and role-playing.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"we demonstrate that removing certain trigger tokens in harmful queries can mitigate these directions to bypass the learned safety capability","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
 {"index":18,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.22061","title":"On the Failure of Topic-Matched Contrast Baselines in Multi-Directional Refusal Abliteration (Petrov 2026)",
  "summary":"Qwen 3.5 2B: topic-matched contrast pairs produce NO functional refusal directions at any weight level or layer; unmatched contrast achieves complete refusal elimination on six layers. Geometric cause: topic-matched subtraction cancels the shared dominant component. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":["Victor Petrov"],"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"It was found that topic-matched contrast produces no functional refusal directions at any tested weight level on any tested layer, while unmatched contrast on the same model, same extraction code, and same evaluation protocol achieves complete refusal elimination on six layers.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"The geometric analysis of the failure establishes that topic-matched subtraction cancels the dominant activation component shared between harmful and harmless prompts of the same subject, reducing the extracted direction magnitude below the threshold at which weight-matrix projection perturbs the residual stream.","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
 {"index":19,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09434","title":"A Behavioral Fingerprint for Large Language Models: Provenance Tracking via Refusal Vectors (arXiv:2602.09434)",
  "summary":"Refusal-vector behavioral fingerprints for IP/provenance: robust to finetunes/merges/quantization; 100% base-family identification across 76 offspring models; degrade but leave traces under alignment-breaking attacks. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"In a large-scale identification task across 76 offspring models, our method achieves 100% accuracy in identifying the correct base model family.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"We demonstrate that these behavioral fingerprints are highly robust against common modifications, including finetunes, merges, and quantization.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"we analyze the fingerprint's behavior under alignment-breaking attacks, finding that while performance degrades significantly, detectable traces remain","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
 {"index":20,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14147","title":"Breaking Refusal in the First Half: A Mechanistic Study of the Prefill Jailbreak (arXiv:2607.14147)",
  "summary":"One-line prefill ('Sure, here is') strips refusal while harm stays internally decodable (probe 0.91-0.98); refusal localized to the first half of the response (dose-matched position control); first-half window is causal. Fetched 2026-09-19.",
  "authors":None,"year":2026,
  "supporting_passages":[
   {"quote":"on the prompts the attack flips to compliance, a linear probe reads harm as high as on the refused ones (0.91-0.98), while behavioral refusal drops to chance. This holds across four models and three families (1.5-3.8B, and at 14B). Refusal is therefore a shallow, response-site computation.","locator":"arXiv abstract"},
   {"quote":"We localize it to an early window: a dose-matched position control shows the first half of the response suffices to break refusal, while the second half is nearly inert.","locator":"arXiv abstract"}]},
